+      "title": "Exact rational cover times by visited-subset recursion",
+      "statement": "For a finite irreducible rational n-state transition matrix P, set V_full=0. For each nonempty proper visited set S, solve (I-P_SS)V_S=1+(sum_(v outside S)P_uv V_(S union {v})(v))_(u in S), in descending size of S. The killed matrix has spectral radius less than one. V_{ {u} }(u) is the exact cover time from u, and their maximum is the worst-start cover time. The method uses O(2^n*n^3) rational arithmetic operations and does not give a polynomial-time approximation algorithm.",
+      "proof_text": "# C0116: exact exponential cover-time oracle\n\nStatus: proof written; w346 KEEP pending. This is an exact finite-instance oracle, not a deterministic polynomial-time (1+epsilon) algorithm.\n\nLet P be a finite irreducible rational transition matrix on n states. For a nonempty visited set S and current state u in S, let V_S(u) be the expected remaining number of steps until every state has been visited. Set V_full=0. For every proper S solve\n\n    (I-P_SS)*V_S = 1 + [sum_{v outside S} P_uv*V_(S union {v})(v)]_(u in S).\n\nEvaluate visited sets in descending cardinality. The exact answer starting at u is V_{ {u} }(u), and the worst-start cover time is their maximum. This takes O(2^n*n^3) rational arithmetic operations; no polynomial bit-complexity bound is claimed.\n\nProof: the right-hand values concern larger visited sets and are already known. First-step conditioning gives the equation. The killed matrix P_SS has spectral radius less than one: from each state in S, irreducibility provides an at-most-n-step positive-probability path to the complement; taking the minimum of finitely many such probabilities yields a geometric exit bound. Therefore I-P_SS is invertible and its inverse is sum_{k>=0}P_SS^k. This proves existence, uniqueness and the recursion by descending induction. Rational elimination gives exact rational expectations.\n\nIndependent controls for simple random walks:\n\n* Complete graph K_n, n>=2: (n-1)*H_(n-1) from every start, because with k visited vertices the probability of a new one is (n-k)/(n-1).\n* A path of L edges: expected cover time from i is L^2+i(L-i), by first hitting an endpoint and then the other. Hence worst-start cover time L^2+floor(L^2/4).\n* Star with k leaves, k>=2: 2k*H_k-1 from the center and 2k*H_(k-1) from a leaf. Coupon trials are center-to-leaf excursions of length two except the final step. For k=1 both starts have cover time one; that boundary case must be handled separately.\n\nThe cached earlier attempt's GFF asymptotic discussion gives no effective finite-instance error bound or deterministic Gaussian-maximum algorithm. This oracle permits exact checks of proposed approximations on small graphs while keeping that unresolved requirement explicit. Its computational approach is classical and no novelty claim is made.\n\nChecks: exact Fraction arithmetic against the three controls, and Bellman residuals on all connected labeled graphs up to four vertices. The checks deliberately keep the exponential algorithm outside any polynomial-time claim.\n",
